        <section id="coding-types"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>Coding types</h2>
              <p>Scroll through the slides below to learn more about the different types of coding.</p>

              
              
<div class="gallery-container">
  <div role="group" class="gallery no-js">
    <ul>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Functional coding
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>A type of declarative paradigm composed of functions which have no effect on variables which are not part of the function.</p>
<p>As a declarative language, it focuses on “what to solve” rather than the imperative “how to solve”, through the use of expressions, rather than statements.</p>
<p>A key point is that functions take in EXISTING inputs (values, data structures, or functions) and return NEW values or data structures.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/uc/uc_u3_s1_p3_slide1.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Object-oriented coding
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Object-oriented programming (OOP) is a type of imperative programming.</p>
<p>What distinguishes OOP is that it focuses on “objects” (a collection of data and functions) which change state, rather than actions which operate on separate pools of data.</p>
<p>Commonly, the objects are “classes”. These are data structures definitions which contain inbuilt data and class-specific functions (known as methods).  <em>We will be looking at Classes later on in Unit 4.</em></p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/uc/uc_u3_s1_p3_slide2.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Procedural code
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>As previously described, type of imperative language. Composed of a set of procedures that run in a pre-determined order.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/uc/uc_u3_s1_p3_slide3.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Logic programming
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>A type of declarative paradigm based on formal logic. Rules and details are based on mathematical system of reasoning.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/uc/uc_u3_s1_p3_slide4.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Parallel process
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>A type of imperative paradigm. It allows processes to run concurrently rather than in sequence (sequential programming) like more fundamental paradigms.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/uc/uc_u3_s1_p3_slide5.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Database processing approach
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Database processing approach is declarative. Users can create and edit database systems using declarative control statements. They can also make queries to retrieve results matching specific criteria.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/uc/uc_u3_s1_p3_slide6.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Event-driven programming
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Technically both imperative and declarative languages support this approach. This is where the control flow of a program is determined by events (such as change in value of a variable) which determine the subsequent processes.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/uc/uc_u3_s1_p3_slide7.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
