          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Jack:</strong> So, what are the advantages and disadvantages of functional, object oriented and other coding types.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Let’s start by looking at Functional coding</p>
<p>The Advantages of Functional coding are:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>It’s easy to debug [no side effects or hidden inputs or outputs]</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Functions are easier to understand because they do not affect any variables outside of themself.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>The Disadvantages of Functional coding are:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>The type of function used can reduce the readability of the code.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Using recursive functions instead of loops can take a while to learn.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Jack:</strong> Now let’s look at Object Oriented Programming [OOP]</p>
<p>The Advantages of Object Oriented Programming are:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>It is very mature and professionally appreciated.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>It is also good for making sophisticated software.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>The Disadvantages of Object Oriented Programming are:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>It involves a steep learning curve.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Large programs end up with slower operating speeds.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Let’s look at some other coding types.</p>
<p>We have already looked at how imperative and declarative coding differs so let’s now look at the advantages and disadvantages of imperative and declarative coding.</p>
<p><strong>Jack:</strong> Imperative coding is a relatively simple concept, and it simplifies code by reusing code sections, however, there is a higher risk of making errors when compared with declarative and it can quickly become complicated. This makes maintenance, optimisation, and extension difficult.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Declarative coding is clearer than imperative coding and we have mentioned is easier to extend, but it can be more difficult to understand for someone who is not a domain specific specialist.</p>
<p><strong>Jack:</strong> With Procedural coding it is easy to track the flow of a program and it is also easy to implement, debug and adapt. However, it can be hard to write and far abstracted from reality.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Logic programming is a rather high level of coding which has good memory management being fast and efficient. But it can be difficult to apply in more general contexts of computation.</p>
<p><strong>Jack:</strong> Parallel processing is more efficient than with sequential programming as it reaches the outcome quicker, but it can be more complex to write, difficult to scale and has potentially higher resource requirements.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
